# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: crash
# An application that dies says so, and leaves what it was doing behind.
#
# An application runs in a process of its own, so when one fails there
# is nothing on screen to look at -- the window it never opened.  What
# is asked here is that the shell notices, names the program and the
# signal, and that the program itself left a file saying where it was.
wait 40
click 980 30
wait 10
menu-pick Utilities
wait 10
note it fails inside start(), before it has a window at all
menu-pick Fault
wait 200
note the shell noticed, and says WHICH program and WHAT killed it
expect-alert Fault.so stopped: signal 11
note and the program left its own account: the signal, and the place
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-crash.txt SIGSEGV
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-crash.txt fault_where_it_starts
note the parent independently records the exact failed child
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-application-exit.txt Fault.so
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-application-exit.txt SIGSEGV
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-application-exit.txt child report:
note and the last thing it did, near the top where it is read first
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_reports/latest-crash.txt the last thing it did
quit
